#* {{RQ:New Yorker|author=w:Louis Menand|title=Literary Hoaxes and the Ethics of Authorship|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230327065045/https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2018/12/10/literary-hoaxes-and-the-ethics-of-authorship|date=3 December 2018|passage=In academic discourse, hybridity is out; intersectionality is in. People are imagined as the sum of their race, gender, sexuality, '''ableness''', and other identities.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=David Kaplan|title=Mastercard's Touch Card Continues Brand's Marketing Efforts to Engage All Consumers—and Their Senses|work=w:Adweek|url=https://www.adweek.com/brand-marketing/mastercards-touch-card-continues-brands-marketing-efforts-to-engage-all-consumers-and-their-senses/|archiveurl=https://archive.ph/dRFFB|archivedate=23 December 2023|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=Adweek, LLC|date=26 October 2021|issn=0199-2864|oclc=1409745535|passage=The card's release is presented as part of a broader effort to demonstrate that inclusiveness, and addressing discrimination across race, sex or degrees of '''ableness''' isn't just a social marketing good—it's good for business.}}
